#: Rows migrated under #550 that have **no** chain yet to assert on, with
#: why. Carried so the set of migrated rows is one list rather than two,
#: and so a row cannot quietly drop out of the instrument's view.
NO_CHAIN_YET = {
    # **Two clauses are written, not one, and it still belongs here.** The
    # old reason said "a chain of one and nothing yet to be exclusive
    # against"; the body declares `f993` *and* `f977`. Probed as a
    # migrated row and the chain rule went **red**: `[] of
    # ['f993', 'f977']` -- 0 of 2 branches pay on this board, because both
    # are trades and `c.choose` takes its first option, which is dealing
    # the damage. So the conclusion was right and the reason was wrong,
    # which is the shape worth catching: an entry can stay correct while
    # describing something that is no longer true. #550.
    "p992": "two Arena Fighting clauses are written (f993 #780, f977) and"
            " neither is reachable here: both forgo damage, and `c.choose`"
            " takes the first option, which is to deal it."
            " `drivers.damage_forgone_for_a_rider` exercises the trade"
            " instead. f3188 is unwritten, as are both Lesser Styles."
            " **f987 is written** -- this said otherwise until 2026-11-03 --"
            " but it does not join the chain and is not a stacking rider"
            " either: `c.as_basic(\"p992\", window=\"charge\")` files an"
            " offer that this row may be used in place of a melee basic"
            " attack, which is a usage window and not a clause that pays"
            " beside one. Its three *unbracketed* clauses stack and are not"
            " a chain",
}

#: Feats of the exclusive category that name a migrated row and are **still
#: riders**, so that row's chain does not cover them and they stack when the
#: printed rule says they must not.
#:
#: **This exists so the gap is in the instrument and not only in a
#: docstring.** A chain that looks complete and is not is the worst of the
#: three states, so every entry here is reported as a skip.
#:
#: **Empty since #556, and `p1758` is why it existed.** That row was the
#: first place #550's convention could not deliver its own main argument:
#: `f981`'s clause asks whether the target has hurt the character since the
#: end of their last turn, and that was a *memory* -- a set filled by
#: watchers, armed by `_riders` through `setup=` at the start of the fight.
#: A body runs far too late to build it, so the clause stayed a rider and
#: stacked with whichever of `f978`/`f985` paid.
#:
#: `c.hurt_me_since` answers the same question off `world.bus.log`, through
#: `query.damaged_since`, with `since_turn_ended` as the boundary. **There
#: is nothing to arm**, so a body is no longer too late and `f981` moved
#: into the chain. The whole memory apparatus in `feats/exploits.py` went
#: with it -- the watchers, the per-creature set, and the `#262` id-reuse
#: hazard its note was written about.
#:
#: Kept rather than deleted: the next migrated row that cannot move a feat
#: belongs here, and an empty dict says "none known" where a missing one
#: would say nothing at all.
#:
#: **No longer empty, and the 2026-11-03 wave is why.** That wave chained
#: ten more rows, and partial migration is the normal state of every one of
#: them: `_riders` arms **one watcher per feat and gates nothing**, so a
#: bracketed feat left as a rider stacks with whichever branch of the body's
#: chain pays. Exclusivity on a row therefore arrives only with its **last**
#: migration, and until then the row is honestly half-covered.
#:
#: That is not a regression the wave introduced -- before it, *every*
#: bracketed clause on these rows was a rider and nothing was exclusive of
#: anything. A chain makes the migrated clauses exclusive of each other and
#: leaves the rest where they were.
#:
#: **Derived by mechanism, not by mention, and three passes were wrong before
#: this one.** A feat belongs here only if it *stacks*. Excluded, after
#: reading each body: `f984`->`p1000` and `f994`->`p919` are `swaps=`
#: entries, `f976`->`p971` is a `c.pre_empt`, and `f987`->`p992` is a
#: `c.as_basic` usage offer -- all four substitute or file an offer rather
#: than paying alongside. An earlier pass that counted any code mention of
#: the ref had all four in the list; a pass before that walked only
#: `_riders` keys and so missed `f986`, which rides `p1000` and `p4542`
#: **hand-written**, with the ref in a guard (`ev.power == "p4542"`) and the
#: payout a bare `c.shift(1)` that names no ref at all.
#: **Six entries were paid by the `feats/exploits.py` wave and the list
#: shrank from nine rows to nine feats.** `p970`, `p2248` and `p971`'s
#: `f973` came off it because those clauses are in the bodies now, and
#: `p4541` and `p4542` each lost the ones that moved.
#:
#: **`f980` on `p971` is a new entry rather than a new rider**, and it is
#: the one correction this list needed. That feat lengthens `p971`'s walk
#: with `c.extend_move`, which the earlier derivation did not count as
#: paying alongside -- fairly, while the only other Arena Fighting clause
#: reaching that row was `f976`'s substitution, because taking the
#: substitution means the walk never happens and the two could not both
#: pay. `f973` is in the chain now and its clause is about the damage the
#: walk provokes, so a character holding `f973` and `f980` collects both.
#:
#: **Five entries were paid by the `general_e`/`general_g` wave**, which was
#: the last of the four `#550` feat-file waves: `f990` came off `p919` and
#: `p1063`, `f991` off `p917`, and `f993` off `p4369` and `p4541`. `p917`,
#: `p919`, `p1063` and `p4369` have no entry left at all.
#:
#: **That gap is paid now, and it was ten feats rather than seven.** This
#: said *"seven `[Arena Fighting]` feats in `feats/general_q.py` ... not one
#: of the seven is listed here"*, naming `f3188`, `f3210`, `f3230`, `f3234`,
#: `f3245`, `f3246`, `f3249`. It is **ten**: `f3196`, `f3199` and `f3218`
#: reach `_on_hit_with` through a module constant rather than a literal, so
#: a search for the literal form missed them -- the same blind spot that
#: undercounted four other censuses in this tree.
#:
#: Nine of the ten are listed below, with `f3230` the documented exception:
#: its "once per encounter" hold spans the powers it names, so a body
#: cannot hold it (`content/CLAUDE.md`, `#924`).
#:
#: **Two limits of this list that the entries cannot express**, both from
#: the card-reading pass:
#:
#: * `STILL_RIDERS` is consulted **only inside the `MIGRATED` loop**, so an
#:   entry for a row with no chain does nothing. Nine rows these feats name
#:   carry no chain yet -- `p992`, `p10470`, `p10472`, `p10591`, `p10592`,
#:   `p10733`, `p10734`, `p10889`, `p10890` -- and `p992` has two written
#:   Arena Fighting clauses that `f3188` stacks beside. A structural limit
#:   of where the list is read, not a missing entry.
#: * `MIGRATED["p917"]` records a chain of **one** where the body declares
#:   two, so the exclusivity assertion on that row is vacuous whatever is
#:   listed here. `#1187`.
#:
#: Left as found rather than half-corrected: deriving it needs each feat's
